Hi Jeff, first off, thanks a ton for your thoughts.  I really
appreciate this discussion and its clear you've given this topic a lot
of thought.

>1) 8 way direction buttons are often not really 8 way direction
> buttons

I handled this one by having a modifier button combined with the
directional will only move diagonally.  You are right that this is
critical for nethack i.e. avoiding traps.

> 2) Remappable buttons.

This is an excellent idea that I had not considered yet.  I am working
to figure out how to make use of this...

> 3) Auto-prompt.

Again, a great idea.  Actually I think this would be at least possible
to work into nethack.

>4) Mini map

I solved this one by adding a zoom function.  With 32x32 tiles, there
is not much screen area for objects.  Some tilesets still look
reasonable when scaled to 16x16 or even 8x8 and you can fit over half
the level on 320 pixels.  I did find a set of 6x6 tiles that are
surprisingly good and would make a great minimap (53 columns on 320
pixels), although you might still need to scroll a little.

> 5) Noun-Verb Inventory.

I have actually thought about an object-centric command structure and
this would be very convenient, although somewhat difficult to implement
well in nethack.  Its definately possible, but I need to think this
over some more.  The very common things (drop, eat, throw, etc) could
be added without too much trouble, but sometimes you want to do
something out of the ordinary with an object and there should be a
simple way to do it from the inventory as well.

> 6) 2D Menus.

this is pretty much the core of my current interface.  I have a 2D
macro interface that is 3 layers deep and you begin at the center with
the most common commands (like open, search, pickup, drop, etc) and
proceed outward to less common.   With 3 layers deep this gives 85
macro slots (using 4 directions only).  It works very well, but could
be better.  I think the autoprompt may clean up the macro interface
quite a bit if I can get that working.

Never tried CalcRogue, but I will look into it.  I used to play kMoria
on my Zodiac with the MessagEase (www.exideas.com) interface for
command/text input.  kMoria used the "donut" tap for movement.  I liked
it quite a bit, but it took a lot of practice to get good at
MessagEase.  kMoria was missing macros so it eventually got sort of
tedious.  Also it was a little annoying to move from the donut tapping
over to MessagEase tapping.  I totally agree that if you are going
touch interface, it really needs to be 100% touch.  Also, experts
should be able to get the controls down to small gestures to be able to
really crank along.  Much like new users prefering menu commands in a
windows UI vs advanced users learning the hot-keys.

I'll POWDER a try (gotta dust off my nes edition sp) -- it looks great,
and I'm looking forward to experience first hand what you've done as
far as "pushing the interface".  Hmmm... did you get save working?
I've got a supercard....

